Z||000014||filthyscarecrow||02-20-2001||02:06 AM||||i've never been a fan of nascar. but i know what nascar fans are going through. 7 years ago i lost my hero too. only i never got to meet him, i never got to see him race in person. earnhardt was to america what ayrton senna was to the rest of the world. it's really pretty weird- a person dies; one whom most have never met in real life, one who had no clue that most of his fans existed as individuals, yet it's still like losing a friend. media these days bring you farther into drivers lives than ever, and after seeing them for so long, and their faces becoming so familiar, their death feels like losing a good friend. like i said, i wasn't even a fan, but it hit me as hard as senna's death did, and moore's. <BR>i really hope however, for nascar's sake, that there is no public outcry for safety. that's what happened to formula one after senna was killed, and the sport effectively died with him. the cars are horrific to drive (hearsay, unfortunately), incredibly nervous at speed, the tracks are more boring than ever with chicanes everywhere, and the cars only pass each other in the pits. if nascar starts tampering with things, winston cup racing will die with dale earnhardt, just as formula one died with ayrton senna. <BR>people need to remember something: the reason racing is exciting is because the drivers constantly flirt with death and usually win. but sometimes they lose- even the best of them... <P>------------------<BR>allow me to show you my exhaust pipes...||||reg||1
